    {
      "id": "F14",
      "statement": "What tells the alerts apart. As indicators they are alike: 6–8 distinct senders within one hour. The recorded relationships beyond the indicator differ. (a) Onward links: A-28137 has none. A-20636 and A-85921 have single-step paths to one account each, and some of their contributions have no onward link. A-10859 (3 links, branching to 2 end accounts), A-37573 (4 links) and A-83577 (5 links) have multi-step paths that every contribution feeds into. (b) Shared devices: only A-10859, A-37573 and A-83577 have a device shared by accounts on their paths. In the A-37573 case the alerted account and every intermediate account sent from one device, D-45880. (c) Amounts: in the recorded transfers, each onward transfer on the multi-step paths is slightly smaller than the one before it (for example 196000, 191000, 187500, 184000 on A-37573's first sequence, and 172000 down to 161500 on A-83577's). For interpretation: A-28137, and to a lesser degree A-20636 and A-85921, show nothing beyond the fan-in itself. A-37573, A-83577 and A-10859 combine fan-in with multi-step timing links and shared devices, which makes them the stronger candidates for review. These remain recorded relationships, not proof of fraud or common control.",
